Grade 1 collateral ligament sprain from awkward twist to knee
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal collateral joint line tenderness, Pain on stress testing with firm endpoint, Mechanism of awkward weight-bearing twist

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-004
Reason for Visit: Acute knee pain following a twisting injury, requiring assessment for severe ligamentous rupture or fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute twisting knee injury resulting in pain and mild dysfunction. Urgent evaluation was required to exclude complete cruciate rupture or intra-articular fracture, ensuring joint stability and preventing further structural damage prior to daytime GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I twisted my knee awkwardly and now the side of it is sore when I walk.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden twisting mechanism with minor varus/valgus force during weight-bearing. This resulted in focal tenderness localized along the joint line of the affected collateral ligament. Mild localized soft tissue swelling over the ligamentous insertion points is present without a large generalized joint effusion. The patient has maintained the ability to fully bear weight immediately following the injury despite mild discomfort. There is an absence of mechanical catching, locking, or clicking during movement.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently with a minor limp.
Social/Safety Concerns: Stairs in the home present a mild navigational challenge.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/82 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, comfortable at rest, exhibiting mild discomfort on weight-bearing pivot movements.
Physical Exam: Knee examination reveals focal collateral joint line tenderness. There is pain on stress testing with a firm endpoint appreciated during varus/valgus stress testing at 30 degrees of knee flexion, indicating structural continuity. Normal pain-free active and passive knee extension and flexion arcs are present. Lachman and anterior drawer tests are negative, indicating intact cruciate ligaments.
Environmental Observations: Hardwood floors without severe hazards. Handrails present on stairs.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 collateral ligament sprain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Meniscal tear - Ruled out due to: Absence of mechanical locking, clicking, and a negative McMurray test.

Anterior cruciate ligament tear - Ruled out due to: Negative Lachman test, absence of a loud "pop" or massive immediate hemarthrosis.

Tibial plateau fracture - Ruled out due to: Ability to comfortably bear weight, lack of severe bony tenderness, and Ottawa Knee Rules not being met.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute traumatic mechanism warranted immediate clinical assessment to rule out severe internal derangement or fracture. Bedside examination confidently demonstrated isolated low-grade collateral ligament pathology with intact structural integrity. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically necessary to exclude conditions requiring immediate orthopedic immobilization, guide acute weight-bearing safety, and prevent secondary joint damage from unprotected mobilization prior to the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommended standard weight-bearing knee radiographs only if pain escalates or weight-bearing becomes impossible. Initiated acute RICE protocol (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation).
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a compression bandage and elevate the leg to decrease local hydrostatic pressure and swelling. This will directly translate to reduced pain and faster return to normal ambulation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for functional reassessment and physiotherapy referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department if you become completely unable to put weight on the leg, if the knee suddenly gives way, or if massive swelling develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; severe occult injuries can sometimes be masked by acute muscle spasm.
Clinical Course: Mild swelling and pain will persist for 1 to 2 weeks. Full ligamentous healing typically requires 4 to 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if your knee locks completely, swells up like a balloon, or if your calf becomes hot, swollen, and severely painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Compression and elevation minimize fluid accumulation in the joint tissues, reducing stiffness and pain.
Actionable Steps: Keep the leg elevated above heart level when seated. Wear the compression bandage during the day but remove it at night.

